      "message": "This change disables zero-copy wrapping for any gralloc buffer whose rowInc differs from align(width, 2) * bytesPerSample when the client requests COLOR_FormatYUV420Planar, COLOR_FormatYUV420SemiPlanar or COLOR_FormatYUVP010. On every device whose gralloc aligns strides (not only NVIDIA), that forces a full-frame CPU copy on every input/output buffer — including for clients that already honor KEY_STRIDE / KEY_SLICE_HEIGHT and would have been correct with the wrapped buffer.\n\nThe commit message also states:\n\n\"When a hardware codec produces output buffers with alignment padding (rowInc !\u003d calculated stride), the converter still chose the wrap path but described the resulting MediaImage to the consumer using the calculated stride.\"\n\nThat does not match what GraphicView2MediaImageConverter actually does (line numbers refer to the base revision):\n\nThe calculated unpadded stride / vStride used in the switch (copyFormat) block (lines 950–1125) only initializes mediaImage-\u003emPlane[] for the fallback back-buffer copy path.\nWhen wrapping succeeds (lines 1229–1243), every mediaImage-\u003emPlane[i] is overwritten with the actual hardware layout: plane.rowInc, plane.colInc, and mView.data()[i] - minPtr.\nCCodecBuffers::handleImageData() and CCodec::configure() then read that MediaImage2 and publish KEY_STRIDE (\u003d mPlane[0].mRowInc), KEY_SLICE_HEIGHT (\u003d (mPlane[1].mOffset - mPlane[0].mOffset) / stride) and image-data to the client. RawGraphicOutputBuffersTest.FlexYuvColorFormat exercises exactly this: it verifies pixel data through the image-data plane offsets / rowInc of a wrapped gralloc block.\n\nSo the padded layout is already described correctly to the client; the wrap path does not mis-describe anything. Note also that the failing scenario is the encoder input path (blocks allocated by CCodec from gralloc via GraphicInputBuffers), not codec-produced output buffers — which is why the software c2.android.* encoders fail on the same device.\n\nFor the CTS failure in b/447580666\n the cause is on the caller side: VideoCodecTestBase\u0027s encoder path writes a packed width * height * 3/2 frame into getInputBuffer() and never reads KEY_STRIDE / KEY_SLICE_HEIGHT from the input format (its decoder path already does). That is a test bug and should be fixed in CTS, not compensated for in the framework.\n\nFor the ByteBuffer-mode apps mentioned in comment #20 of the bug: an app that assumes stride \u003d\u003d width for COLOR_FormatYUV420Planar / SemiPlanar without reading KEY_STRIDE / KEY_SLICE_HEIGHT (or using getInputImage() / getOutputImage()) is likewise a caller bug — MediaImage2 is how the layout is communicated, and a 256-byte-aligned gralloc is exactly the case it exists to describe. If an app does read KEY_STRIDE / KEY_SLICE_HEIGHT (or MediaImage2) and still sees corruption, please share the buffer\u0027s C2PlanarLayout (rowInc and plane offsets for Y/U/V) and the resulting MediaImage2 / KEY_* values, so we can look for a genuine description gap (e.g. inter-plane padding that KEY_SLICE_HEIGHT cannot represent).\n\nFinally, even on its own terms the change is incomplete: it validates only horizontal rowInc, not the plane offsets, so a height-padded buffer (e.g. 1080 → 1088 rows with an already-aligned width) still wraps and a packed-assuming client still breaks; and COLOR_FormatYUVP210 is skipped. That reinforces that gating zero-copy on a synthesized stride is the wrong layer rather than something to extend.\n\nI\u0027m therefore not in favor of this approach. -1.",
